WEBVTT

1
00:00:14.308 --> 00:00:19.512
had just come home from school when she heard a light hammering sound coming from Grandpa's study.

2
00:00:21.213 --> 00:00:23.034
Curious, she tapped on the door.

3
00:00:23.054 --> 00:00:25.436
"Grandpa? Can I come in?"

4
00:00:26.617 --> 00:00:27.537
"Just a second!"

5
00:00:28.058 --> 00:00:28.998
Grandpa answered.

6
00:00:29.599 --> 00:00:31.200
Laurie heard lots of shuffling.

7
00:00:31.704 --> 00:00:38.888
"Enter!" Grandpa was sitting empty-handed at his desk, but there were wood shavings everywhere.

8
00:00:38.908 --> 00:00:42.010
"Aren't you supposed to be at school?"

9
00:00:42.350 --> 00:00:42.911
he asked.

10
00:00:42.931 --> 00:00:45.072
"It's over at three.

11
00:00:45.652 --> 00:00:46.973
What were you doing in here?"

12
00:00:47.594 --> 00:00:49.014
Laurie said suspiciously.

13
00:00:49.695 --> 00:00:52.737
She saw something wooden poking out from underneath a book.

14
00:00:52.757 --> 00:00:56.519
"Aha!" she cried, pulling the object out.

15
00:00:57.159 --> 00:00:58.040
"What's this?"

16
00:00:59.388 --> 00:01:02.990
"Well, Miss Nosy, that's your birthday present.

17
00:01:03.630 --> 00:01:06.512
But now it won't be a surprise," Grandpa said.

18
00:01:07.232 --> 00:01:11.935
It was a wooden crocodile with a string attached to the top of its head so the mouth

19
00:01:11.995 --> 00:01:13.336
could be opened and shut.

20
00:01:14.096 --> 00:01:17.598
Laurie pulled the string and snapped the crocodile's jaws.

21
00:01:17.618 --> 00:01:22.021
"It's an ancient Egyptian toy," Grandpa said.

22
00:01:22.041 --> 00:01:27.784
"I made this, but if you were a child in ancient Egypt, you might have something similar.

23
00:01:28.488 --> 00:01:36.093
Crocodiles were a significant part of Egyptian life because the civilization was built along the Nile River, and

24
00:01:36.173 --> 00:01:38.175
many crocodiles lived there.

25
00:01:38.995 --> 00:01:46.060
The Egyptian civilization has been called the gift of the Nile, because the Egyptians depended on the river

26
00:01:46.100 --> 00:01:49.803
for many things, including irrigation and fertilizer.

27
00:01:50.824 --> 00:01:56.748
It flooded every year, supplying much-needed water and nutrients to the surrounding land.

28
00:01:57.600 --> 00:02:00.042
This made farming easy for the Egyptians.

29
00:02:00.583 --> 00:02:05.528
The soft, wet land also made good mud for building mud-brick houses.

30
00:02:06.188 --> 00:02:13.575
And the river unified the people of the area by providing a convenient highway for transportation and communication.

31
00:02:14.856 --> 00:02:17.679
Ancient Egypt was an agricultural society.

32
00:02:18.140 --> 00:02:19.100
Do you know what that means?

33
00:02:19.641 --> 00:02:22.584
It means life depended heavily on farming.

34
00:02:23.112 --> 00:02:29.116
If you were a little Egyptian girl living about four thousand years ago, your father would probably be

35
00:02:29.176 --> 00:02:30.016
a grain farmer.

36
00:02:30.757 --> 00:02:37.721
Grains were the most common crop, especially wheat and barley, which were used to make bread and beer—the

37
00:02:37.761 --> 00:02:39.422
most typical food and drink.

38
00:02:40.503 --> 00:02:44.545
Unfortunately children didn't get to go to school like you do now.

39
00:02:45.206 --> 00:02:48.288
You'd probably spend most days helping your parents.

40
00:02:48.940 --> 00:02:52.941
During harvest season maybe you would bundle wheat on the farm.

41
00:02:53.761 --> 00:02:58.383
Or perhaps you'd be at home, helping to cook or take care of the vegetable garden near your

42
00:02:58.423 --> 00:03:01.944
house. But Egypt wasn't all farms.

43
00:03:02.324 --> 00:03:08.646
If you were a well-to-do Egyptian family, you might live near a city," Grandpa said, looking around his

44
00:03:08.706 --> 00:03:13.087
desk. He handed Laurie a drawing of an ancient Egyptian city.

45
00:03:13.107 --> 00:03:15.008
"What's this big thing?"

46
00:03:15.608 --> 00:03:19.945
Laurie asked, pointing to a large structure rising above palm trees.

47
00:03:19.965 --> 00:03:23.786
"And what's that funny picture of a bird with a beach ball?"

48
00:03:25.266 --> 00:03:29.728
"That's the temple to the sun god, Ra.

49
00:03:30.508 --> 00:03:37.250
He was the most important god, as the Egyptians thought he was responsible for the rising sun, which

50
00:03:37.330 --> 00:03:39.430
brought light and nourishment to their land.

51
00:03:40.291 --> 00:03:42.771
Many temples were built in worship to him.

52
00:03:43.171 --> 00:03:45.332
And that's not a bird with a beach ball!

53
00:03:46.036 --> 00:03:49.918
That's ‘Ra' in hieroglyphics, an ancient form of writing.

54
00:03:50.959 --> 00:03:56.202
Just as the sun looks different throughout the day, Ra appeared in many different forms.

55
00:03:57.002 --> 00:04:03.406
But most often he was shown as a falcon carrying the great, big sun over his head.

56
00:04:04.166 --> 00:04:09.389
Every morning he rose from underground and traveled across the sky in a boat.

57
00:04:10.109 --> 00:04:13.231
And every evening he went back beneath the ground.

58
00:04:14.764 --> 00:04:18.286
He had the most important job of all—creator of the universe.

59
00:04:19.587 --> 00:04:23.989
The next most important job was that of the pharaoh, the Egyptian king.

60
00:04:24.530 --> 00:04:26.931
Pharaohs were considered part god.

61
00:04:27.812 --> 00:04:32.934
They were in charge of politics, the military, and the welfare of their citizens.

62
00:04:33.775 --> 00:04:36.737
They were also the link between the gods and humans.

63
00:04:37.757 --> 00:04:42.180
Without the pharaoh, Egyptians thought, the world would end up in chaos!

64
00:04:43.032 --> 00:04:46.894
But the pharaohs lived a life of luxury compared to common farmers.

65
00:04:48.054 --> 00:04:54.898
They owned most of the land, lived in huge palaces with many servants, and could get whatever they

66
00:04:54.938 --> 00:05:00.560
wanted. Both their responsibilities and wealth continued after death too.

67
00:05:01.301 --> 00:05:06.943
When a pharaoh died, Egyptians believed he became the king of the dead and buried him in an

68
00:05:07.043 --> 00:05:08.124
elaborate tomb.

69
00:05:10.428 --> 00:05:15.610
In order for him to be able to perform his duties properly, he had to be buried with

70
00:05:15.990 --> 00:05:17.131
everything he would need.

71
00:05:17.771 --> 00:05:22.533
This included his body, which was preserved, and his most valuable possessions.

72
00:05:23.173 --> 00:05:29.036
Inside the tomb the walls were also covered with paintings of slaves, food, and animals.

73
00:05:29.696 --> 00:05:32.777
You've seen pictures of the pyramids of Giza, right?

74
00:05:34.538 --> 00:05:38.440
Those magnificent structures were tombs for pharaohs!

75
00:05:39.692 --> 00:05:43.095
The tomb paintings have taught us a lot about the Egyptians.

76
00:05:43.675 --> 00:05:46.818
For example, one of their favorite sports was hunting.

77
00:05:47.379 --> 00:05:51.602
Egyptians are thought to be the first people to hunt purely for pleasure.

78
00:05:52.083 --> 00:06:00.089
The area around the Nile was full of wildlife; they hunted birds, gazelles, lions, and hippopotamuses!

79
00:06:00.990 --> 00:06:03.813
Hippo hunts seem to have been particularly popular.

80
00:06:04.813 --> 00:06:07.616
Hippos, you know, live mostly in the water.

81
00:06:08.560 --> 00:06:12.062
Egyptians hunted them with long spears from boats.

82
00:06:12.942 --> 00:06:13.983
Hippo ahead!"

83
00:06:14.523 --> 00:06:18.485
Grandpa cried, jumping up and pretending to spot something in the distance.

84
00:06:18.505 --> 00:06:23.668
"Attack!" he yelled as he hurled an imaginary spear into the air.

85
00:06:23.688 --> 00:06:26.770
"Grandpa, did they hunt crocodiles?"

86
00:06:27.250 --> 00:06:30.472
Laurie asked worriedly, holding the toy on her palm.

87
00:06:31.412 --> 00:06:35.134
Grandpa returned from his fantasy hippo hunt and looked at Laurie.

88
00:06:35.154 --> 00:06:42.050
"Well, I haven't seen any pictures of it, but hunting dangerous animals was a way to earn respect.

89
00:06:42.590 --> 00:06:44.592
And crocodiles are pretty dangerous.

90
00:06:45.192 --> 00:06:50.935
But crocodiles were associated with the water god, whom Egyptians both feared and honored.

91
00:06:51.555 --> 00:06:55.638
So perhaps your little crocodile would have been spared."

92
00:06:56.618 --> 00:06:59.600
Grandpa smiled and tapped the crocodile on its head.

93
00:06:59.960 --> 00:07:01.121
Laurie smiled too.

94
00:07:01.141 --> 00:07:03.522
"Laurie! I'm home!"

95
00:07:03.922 --> 00:07:05.483
Mom called from downstairs.

96
00:07:05.503 --> 00:07:06.824
"Hi, Mom!

97
00:07:07.104 --> 00:07:08.185
I'll be right down!"

98
00:07:08.605 --> 00:07:09.705
Laurie hollered back.

99
00:07:09.725 --> 00:07:11.326
"Thanks, Grandpa.

100
00:07:11.726 --> 00:07:17.209
I'll pretend I never saw this," she said, putting the crocodile back on the desk.

101
00:07:17.229 --> 00:07:18.730
"Until next time!"

102
00:07:18.750 --> 00:07:20.651
"Until next time!

103
00:07:21.131 --> 00:07:21.431
Bye,
